              <chunks count="1">
                <chunk name="Container">
                  <items count="8">
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">A panel for custom notes and text values</item>
                    <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">759ba0d8-ae8d-4d3a-ae88-45058724013f</item>
                    <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Panel</item>
                    <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10"></item>
                    <item name="Optional" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">false</item>
                    <item name="ScrollRatio" type_name="gh_double" type_code="6">0</item>
                    <item name="SourceCount" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                    <item name="UserText" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">SOLAR ACCESS
The whole solar chain against an answer you can
work out on paper. Units: METERS.

THE GNOMON ORACLE
The geometry is a single vertical pole: 0.5 m
square section, 10 m tall, standing on the
origin, from TestSolarShadowGeometryE2E. A pole
of height h under a sun at elevation alpha casts
its tip at horizontal distance h / tan(alpha),
directly away from the sun. That is exact
trigonometry with no model in it, and it is the
oldest instrument in the subject.

WHY IT IS WORTH CHECKING: a sign flip on azimuth,
north measured clockwise instead of counter-
clockwise, or x and y transposed all produce
shadows of exactly the right LENGTH pointing in
the wrong DIRECTION - smooth, plausible output,
no error anywhere, and a facade study that
recommends shading the wrong elevation. Watch the
shadow against the Sun Path compass, not against
itself.

SITE Ithaca NY: 42.44 N, -76.50 E, UTC-5. Set on
both Sun Path and Sun Vectors. North 0.

GROUND 40 x 40 m centred on the pole, gridded by
Facade Grid at 1 m with a 0.05 m offset. Max Tilt
90 keeps horizontal faces, which is what makes a
GROUND grid rather than a facade one. The grid
carries per-cell areas, so Sun Stats and Sunlight
Compliance weight by area rather than point count.

*** PERIOD: ONE DAY, NOT ONE YEAR ***
Sun Hours ships at Start 4344 / End 4368 - the
24 hours of 21 June. Widening it to 0 / 8760 is
1,600 points x 8,760 sun samples of ray casting
and turns a seconds-long recompute into a very
long one. Raise Timestep before you raise the
period: an hour moves the sun ~15 deg, so against
a sharp shadow edge an hourly step misses
transitions.

WEATHER: opening this document downloads the
Ithaca EPW from climate.onebuilding.org and
caches it under ~/Eddy3D. Download Weather hands
back a PATH; the Weather component turns that
into the record Sun Hours, Shadow and Solar
Irradiation actually accept. Leaving the Search
panel empty instead makes five components go red.

CANOPY is left unwired on both Sun Hours and
Solar Irradiation. If you wire it, wire it by
NAME, not by remembered index: Sun Hours' input
order changed and old documents get migrated, so
a hard-coded position lands on the wrong socket.

Sunlight Compliance is on EN 17037 (Standard 0).
Design aid - its thresholds are editable and not
certified.

Nothing here writes a file or runs an engine.</item>
